Verdict: FAIL

The diff is well-constructed — pure decision function, one wiring site, union/dedup/idempotence/superset all table-tested, byte-identical near-miss guard, help text and CLAUDE.md moved in the same commit. But it half-works, and the documented half claims otherwise.

What I verified:

• bash is genuinely removed: it comes from build_tools, and agent_builder.rs:907-908 runs `tools.retain(|t| !self.disallowed_tools.contains(...))` over that list.
• sub_agent is not: agent_builder.rs:932 does `tools.push(with_session_cap(sub_agent_tool, ...)) **after** that filter, unconditionally, and build_sub_agent_tool never consults disallowed_tools (grep in src/tools.rs` returns only a test fixture).
• The codebase says so itself, at agent_builder.rs:880: "the sub_agent/shared_state bypass that disallowed_tools couldn't catch (they were added after filtering via with_sub_agent)."

Why that's a FAIL rather than a nit: `RESTRICTED_REMOVED_TOOLS`' own doc comment argues that removing the dispatcher is *"the fail-closed choice and does not rest on that question being answered"* — the question being whether the disallowed list propagates to children. The dispatcher isn't removed, so the clause rests on exactly that unanswered question, and a sub-agent child is built with its own tool set including bash. Meanwhile --help and the startup note now both print command-running tools removed — bash, sub_agent. That is the #879 defect class shipped with the sign flipped: the flag previously failed to confine; it now denies a confinement it does not perform.

All six new tests pass because they pin the pure function's returned Vec. For a tool-removal feature the emission point is the tool list the agent is built with, and nothing asserts on it.

The fix is likely small — gate the two pushes at agent_builder.rs:932-933 on disallowed_tools, or filter after them — but it belongs in the same diff as the help text that promises it.
